    description: "**Per `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bf4oxh91w_investigation_threads_04072026_1702.md` Thread 4 §\"Tier 1 — single coordinated cutover, requires explicit human authorization.\"** Companion to the invariant item *\"Training-data corpus must carry per-entry pipeline/main-llm cohort metadata.\"*\n\n**The cutover sequence** (single coordinated change, in order):\n\n1. Pause `sync-transcript.sh` (whatever currently writes to `.agent/.training-data.jsonl`).\n2. Snapshot `.agent/.training-data.jsonl` → `.agent/.training-data-v0-snapshot-<date>.jsonl`. The snapshot is the v0 corpus; the new pipeline writes v1+.\n3. Add ALL of these fields to `log_training_example` in `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py` in **one** change. None enter the `messages` array; all are top-level metadata:\n   - `pipeline_version: \"v1\"` (forward marker)\n   - `infra_sha` — current git SHA of `on_transcript_change.py` at write time\n   - `playbook_registry_sha` — current git SHA of the file containing the PLAYBOOKS registry\n   - `main_llm` — extracted from the most recent assistant message in the byte window. The raw JSONL has `message.model` per assistant turn. Requires a small refactor in `select_recent_entries` to scan for the most recent assistant message and pull `.message.model` alongside the byte slice.\n   - `vendor: \"claude-code\"`\n   - `vendor_version` — extracted from the JSONL's top-level `version` field\n   - `scoring_model` — alias for the legacy `model` field. Keep `model` as a backward-compat alias for any data loader; new field is `scoring_model`.\n4. Run the backfill script (separate item) against the v0 snapshot to label historical entries with `infra_sha`, `playbook_registry_sha`, presumed `main_llm`, etc. Output goes to a sidecar `.training-data-v0-snapshot-<date>-cohort-tags.jsonl`.\n5. Restart the watcher.\n6. Document in the main `CHANGELOG.md` (the watcher lives at `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py`, not under `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/`, so the main changelog is the right target — verified 2026-04-08 via Glob) — version bump appropriate, new fields enumerated, link to the invariant item being satisfied.\n\n**Why \"all in one change, not piecemeal\":** per the notebook Thread 4 §\"Why 'just add fields whenever the next change happens' is the right plan\":\n- Cutover cost is the same whether you add 1 field or 7. Marginal cost per field is ~0.\n- Cost of NOT capturing a dimension and later wishing you had ≈ \"throw away the corpus.\"\n- Doing it as a single coordinated cutover is cheaper than piecemeal because each piecemeal change has its own pause/snapshot/restart cost.\n\n**Constraint to honor:** the watcher is *continuously running*. Any change touching `filter-transcript.py`, `on_transcript_change.py:select_recent_entries`, the PLAYBOOKS registry, or the prompt templates silently shifts the training distribution mid-corpus. The pause-snapshot-restart sequence is the discipline that makes this safe.\n\n**STATUS: needs_human_review per AGENTS.md governance file list. `.agent/hooks/` is governance.** Specifically:\n- The pause/restart of the watcher affects shared state.\n- The corpus snapshot is irreversible (well, reversible by `rm`, but socially significant).\n- The new fields' design needs human sign-off before they're locked in.\n\n**Approval workflow:** stays in `needs_human_review` until the human approves the design AND explicitly authorizes the cutover window. After approval, an implementation item can be created and submitted as a PR with explicit authorization.\n\n**Pairs with:**\n- The invariant item (`Training-data corpus must carry...`) which this cutover satisfies for v1+ entries\n- The backfill script item which satisfies it for v0 entries\n- The Tier 2 deferred item (`filter-transcript.py` queue-operation normalization) which is explicitly NOT bundled into this cutover — Tier 2 changes need their own justification."  # 6b92
